WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
You have food trucks in your neighborhood?

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:03.120
I do.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.455
We have lots of them, and I like them.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.700
They provide good, different foods at good prices.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.040
Laura Pekarik drives a food truck in Chicago.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.260
She sells cupcakes

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.360
and the truck let her open a business

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:17.880
without having to hire a staff

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:18.480
and

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:19.800
rent a whole building.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.160
I was able to rent a kitchen space

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.380
instead of renting a whole brick and mortar and

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.780
managing a team of people.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.940
It was just me and one baker at the time

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.500
and everything kind of was under my control,

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.920
to kind of get my feet wet in the business.

00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:38.060
Joey Vanoni's a food truck entrepreneur in Baltimore.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:39.260
He sells pizza.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.324
Not just any pizza,

00:00:40.324 --> 00:00:41.880
delicious pizza.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.660
When did you get the idea to open this food truck?

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.400
I was looking around for some jobs

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.040
and I could not find anything.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.620
It's kind of a new idea.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.420
It really started taking off

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:53.760
right around the recession, you know

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.000
2008 timeframe.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.800
A lot of restaurant entrepreneurs,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.540
the capital they had was not enough

00:00:58.540 --> 00:00:59.660
to actually go out and start

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.700
a restaurant on their own.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.220
Banks weren't willing to give loans,

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.480
so the food truck phenomenon really took off.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.720
Food trucks are just a way for people to enter.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.920
Dick Carpenter of the Institute for Justice

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.040
studied food trucks and concluded

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.460
they're an important part of the economy,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.420
because they provide a first rung

00:01:18.429 --> 00:01:20.580
for people starting out.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.009
The costs are often much less for a food truck.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.660
So it's a way for them to get into business

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.180
and then grow that business into something bigger.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.474
But new rules now make that tougher.

00:01:30.474 --> 00:01:32.880
Some cities restrict food truck sales

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.260
to two hours.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.680
Others tell food trucks you may not park

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.140
near an established restaurant.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.320
It became ever more difficult

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.660
to find parking locations when we went to the city

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.000
to try to sell our cupcakes.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.577
I would have customers calling me, trying to find us

00:01:46.577 --> 00:01:49.500
and I was like I'm trying. I'm trying to find a parking spot,

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.840
I'll post as soon as I land.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.700
Every moment that we're driving around

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.340
and not parked in the location with our window open

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.620
meant that we couldn't sell.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.240
Parkings limits it

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.200
because politicians want to protect

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.980
existing restaurants.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.420
And you can't be 300 feet next to a restaurant

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.700
that offers the same product.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.300
Well there are pizzerias all over Baltimore,

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.380
so that means Joey is severely restricted

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:14.520
in where he can go.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:15.753
They're paying taxes.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.820
They had to use brick and mortar.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.060
Baltimore's just trying to keep things fair.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.900
Well, it's funny you say that John

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.800
because I also invested in my business

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.100
and

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.840
I pay taxes as well.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.340
So you can still run your business,

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.120
you just have to stay away from those restaurants.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.360
There's almost no place left for me to operate.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.760
But we must protect existing restaurants,

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.340
says Chicago Alderman Tom Tunney.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.040
I'm going to be prejudicial

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.940
towards those kind of businesses

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.480
because that keeps my neighborhood.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.040
A brick and mortar is a much more stable enterprise.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.960
This is a prime example of protectionism

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.180
Wait, who are these kids

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.480
talking about protectionism?

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:55.380
I'm Ethan

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.600
and I'm Emily.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.420
Together we're the Tuttle Twins.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:00.580
More on the Tuttle Twins in another video.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.800
But, they rightly ask

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.694
"Why would the government favor

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.980
a business over another?"

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.720
That's a good question.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.580
Often times it's because one side

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.000
lobbied for regulations.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.840
Sometimes that side also donated money to a

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.354
politician's campaign.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.300
Man, the government can be so shady.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:19.840
Yes,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.380
it can be.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:22.305
And by the way,

00:03:22.305 --> 00:03:23.600
Tunney the politician

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.707
who's in charge of limiting food truck parking

00:03:25.707 --> 00:03:27.100
in this district

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:30.820
just happens to own some brick and mortar restaurants.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.060
At least he discloses that.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.780
For obvious transparency reasons, I'm a restaurant

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:38.980
operator in the city of Chicago

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.500
for almost 40 years.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.340
They want to keep competitors as far away as possible

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.600
The guy who opened the restaurant

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.629
and had to pay real estate taxes

00:03:47.629 --> 00:03:49.620
and pay for his building,

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.160
isn't he getting ripped off

00:03:51.176 --> 00:03:52.820
by these new guys?

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:55.440
That assumes that the food truck operator doesn't pay

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.120
expenses of the same type.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.540
In fact, food truck operators pay

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.360
taxes, they pay rents,

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.720
and through their rents they pay property taxes.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:06.600
Laura and Joey

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.960
now are fighting the regulations in court.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.600
They argue it's unconstitutional to

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:12.960
favor one industry

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:14.700
at the expense of another.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.900
To try to push us back and down and

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.680
prevent us from doing business is unfair.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.760
This case with Laura,

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.020
five years

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:23.080
and going.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.320
The Restaurant Association

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.160
has so much influence

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.460
over those

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:28.640
who are elected officials.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.487
That gives existing restaurants

00:04:30.487 --> 00:04:31.480
the power

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.120
to stop newcomers

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.260
by imposing lots of rules.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.240
The nerve of them!

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.940
That's getting in the way of new jobs!

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.680
This is true of all bottleneckers.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:42.780
Yes, it is.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.220
Like closing a lane on a highway,

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.400
bottleneckers stop all sorts of progress.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.180
Cosmetologists take jobs from hair braiders,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.100
optometrists ban computer eye tests,

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.540
union bus companies

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.460
ban these minivans,

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:58.780
florists say,

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.540
you may not do flower arrangements without a license.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.440
These bottleneckers say they're protecting us,

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:06.520
but mostly

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:07.740
they collude with government

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.400
to protect themselves.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.480
In this case,

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.340
taking away our choices

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.220
one meal at a time.

